BT /F2 16 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 750.6 Tm (A Short History of Glass) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50.0 724.6 Tm (A) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 724.6 Tm (Long before people learned to make it, they used glass that nature had made. Obsidian, a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 708.7 Tm (black glass that forms when lava cools very quickly, was chipped into knives and arrowheads) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 692.7 Tm (more than ten thousand years ago. Because it breaks into pieces with extremely sharp edges,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 676.8 Tm (it was valued so highly that it was carried and traded hundreds of kilometres from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 660.8 Tm (volcanoes where it was found.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50.0 635.9 Tm (B) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 635.9 Tm (The first glass made by people appeared in Mesopotamia around 3500 BC, probably as a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 619.9 Tm (by-product of metalworking or pottery. For many centuries it was used only for small objects) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 604.0 Tm (such as beads and the shiny coating on clay pots. Making a vessel was slow and costly:) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 588.0 Tm (workers wrapped hot glass around a core of sand and clay, and then scraped the core out) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 572.1 Tm (once the glass had cooled.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50.0 547.1 Tm (C) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 547.1 Tm (Everything changed in the first century BC, when craftsmen on the eastern coast of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 531.2 Tm (Mediterranean discovered that glass could be blown. By blowing air through a metal pipe into) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 515.2 Tm (a small ball of molten glass, a worker could form a thin bottle in minutes. Glass became cheap) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 499.3 Tm (enough for ordinary households, and the Romans spread the technique to every corner of) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 483.3 Tm (their empire.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50.0 458.4 Tm (D) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 458.4 Tm (In medieval Europe, glassmakers were both admired and controlled. In Venice the industry) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 442.4 Tm (was moved to the island of Murano in 1291, officially to protect the city from fire. The move) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 426.5 Tm (also kept the secrets of the trade in one place: skilled workers were treated as important) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 410.5 Tm (citizens, but they were not allowed to leave the republic.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 50.0 385.6 Tm (E) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 385.6 Tm (Clear window glass remained a luxury until the nineteenth century. Early panes were made by) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 369.6 Tm (spinning a disc of glass, which left them thick in the middle and uneven at the edges. In 1959,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 353.7 Tm (the British engineer Alastair Pilkington introduced the float process, in which molten glass is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 337.7 Tm (poured onto a bath of liquid tin. The result is a perfectly flat sheet, and almost all window glass) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 80.0 321.8 Tm (is still made this way today.) Tj ET
