BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 771.1 Tm (You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 14–26, which are based on Reading Passage 2 below.) Tj ET
BT /F2 16 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 750.6 Tm (Why We Sleep) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 724.6 Tm (For most of history, sleep was seen as a time when the brain simply switched off. That view began) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 708.7 Tm (to change in 1953, when Eugene Aserinsky, a student in Chicago, noticed that the eyes of sleeping) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 692.7 Tm (people moved quickly behind their closed lids. These periods of rapid eye movement, or REM) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 676.8 Tm (sleep, turned out to be the time when most dreaming happens, and they showed that the sleeping) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 660.8 Tm (brain is far from idle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 635.9 Tm (Later research has linked sleep to memory. The psychologist Matthew Walker has shown that) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 619.9 Tm (people who sleep after learning a list of words remember up to forty per cent more than people who) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 604.0 Tm (stay awake. According to Walker, the brain uses deep sleep to move new information from a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 588.0 Tm (short-term store into long-term memory, rather like saving a document before closing the computer.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 563.1 Tm (Sleep also seems to clean the brain. In 2013 the neuroscientist Maiken Nedergaard found that the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 547.1 Tm (spaces between brain cells grow larger during sleep, allowing fluid to wash away waste products.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 531.2 Tm (Some of these waste products are connected with illnesses that affect memory in old age, which) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 515.2 Tm (suggests that poor sleep over many years may have serious effects.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 490.3 Tm (Teenagers face a particular problem. Their internal clock shifts later during adolescence, so many) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 474.3 Tm (of them are not tired until eleven o’clock at night or later. Schools that start at eight in the morning) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 458.4 Tm (therefore force them to wake in the middle of their natural night. Several studies in the United) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 442.4 Tm (States found that when schools moved their start time later, attendance improved and fewer) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 426.5 Tm (students fell asleep in class.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 401.5 Tm (Despite this evidence, adults in many countries now sleep less than their grandparents did.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 385.6 Tm (Artificial light, long working hours and the glow of phone screens all push bedtime later. Doctors) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 369.6 Tm (recommend between seven and nine hours a night for adults, yet surveys suggest that about a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.0 353.7 Tm (third regularly get fewer than six.) Tj ET
